('bis-seagate-2023','enforcement_action','BIS imposes $300 million civil penalty on Seagate for hard drive shipments to Huawei','Seagate Technology LLC and Seagate Singapore International Headquarters Pte. Ltd. — Order Relating to Settlement',
'BIS reached a $300 million administrative settlement with Seagate over shipments of hard disk drives to Huawei-related entities in apparent violation of the foreign direct product rule.',
'On 19 April 2023 BIS announced a $300 million civil penalty against Seagate Technology LLC and Seagate Singapore International Headquarters Pte. Ltd. for allegedly selling over 7.4 million hard disk drives to Huawei entities between August 2020 and September 2021, in violation of the foreign direct product rule applicable to Entity List entries. The settlement includes a multi-year audit requirement and a suspended denial order. Status: administrative settlement; conduct described as alleged.',
'The case remains the largest standalone BIS administrative penalty to date and illustrates application of the foreign direct product rule to non-US manufactured items produced with US technology.',
'ANALYSIS — Manufacturers should map US-origin technology and tooling used in foreign production to assess FDPR exposure. Continuing to supply a customer after competitors withdraw is a red flag BIS explicitly considered.',
'2023-04-19','2023-04-19',null,(select id from jurisdictions where code='US'),(select id from regimes where slug='multi'),(select id from authorities where slug='bis'),
'{"Seagate Technology LLC","Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd."}','{"Semiconductors","Electronics"}','{"Export Administration Regulations, 15 CFR §734.9(e)","15 CFR §744.11"}',
null,300000000,'USD','settlement','Exports subject to the foreign direct product rule to Entity List parties without a licence',
'{}','{"Incorrect interpretation of FDPR scope","Continued supply to listed customer"}',
'https://www.bis.gov/press-release/bis-imposes-300-million-penalty-against-seagate-technology-llc-related-shipments-huawei','US Department of Commerce, BIS','verified',false,'{"FDPR","Entity List","Huawei"}'),

('ofac-eo14114-ffi-guidance','guidance','OFAC guidance on secondary sanctions risk for foreign financial institutions under EO 14114','Updated Guidance for Foreign Financial Institutions on OFAC Sanctions Authorities Targeting Support to Russia''s Military-Industrial Base',
'Executive Order 14114 authorised OFAC to impose sanctions, including correspondent account restrictions, on foreign financial institutions that facilitate significant transactions involving Russia''s military-industrial base. OFAC issued accompanying guidance with red flags and expected controls.',
'Executive Order 14114 of 22 December 2023 amended EO 14024. It authorises sanctions on foreign financial institutions that have conducted or facilitated any significant transaction involving persons designated for operating in specified sectors of the Russian economy, or involving the sale, supply or transfer to Russia of specified items. OFAC published guidance and new FAQs the same day, including a list of items such as certain machine tools and electronics, and recommended due diligence measures. The authority was subsequently expanded in June 2024 to cover designated persons under EO 14024 generally.',
'The order creates secondary sanctions exposure for non-US banks with no US nexus in the underlying transaction. The guidance emphasises risk-based controls, trade finance diligence and customer outreach.',
'ANALYSIS — Non-US banks should assess trade finance and correspondent flows for goods on the OFAC/BIS common high-priority list and customers in transshipment hubs. Boards should be informed that loss of US correspondent access is the principal consequence.',
'2023-12-22','2023-12-22','2023-12-22',(select id from jurisdictions where code='US'),(select id from regimes where slug='russia'),(select id from authorities where slug='ofac'),
'{"Foreign financial institutions"}','{"Banking","Trade Finance"}','{"Executive Order 14114","Executive Order 14024"}',
null,null,null,'in_force',null,'{"Third-country transshipment","Front companies"}','{}','https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20231222','US Treasury OFAC','verified',true,'{"secondary sanctions","FFI","military-industrial base"}'),

('tri-seal-third-party-intermediaries','circumvention','Tri-seal compliance note: third-party intermediaries used to evade Russia sanctions and export controls','Cracking Down on Third-Party Intermediaries Used to Evade Russia-Related Sanctions and Export Controls',
'DOJ, Commerce (BIS) and Treasury (OFAC) jointly identified common tactics used to route controlled goods and funds to Russia through third countries and intermediaries, with red-flag indicators for exporters and financial institutions.',
'Joint compliance note published 2 March 2023 by the US Department of Justice, Department of Commerce and Department of the Treasury. It identifies tactics including use of corporate vehicles to obscure beneficial ownership and end use; transshipment through third countries; and the use of intermediaries in jurisdictions such as Armenia, China, Hong Kong, Turkey and the UAE (among others named). It lists red flags such as reluctance to share end-use information, use of shell companies, last-minute changes to shipping routes and payments from third parties. Classification: official risk indicators (not findings in a specific case).',
'The note is the principal US official typology document for Russia-related diversion and is commonly referenced in later enforcement releases.',
'ANALYSIS — Use the red flags to calibrate transaction monitoring scenarios for trade finance and correspondent banking. Note these are official risk indicators; a single indicator does not establish evasion.',
'2023-03-02','2023-03-02',null,(select id from jurisdictions where code='US'),(select id from regimes where slug='russia'),(select id from authorities where slug='ofac'),
'{"Exporters","Freight forwarders","Financial institutions"}','{"Trade","Logistics","Banking"}','{"Export Administration Regulations","Executive Order 14024"}',
null,null,null,'not_applicable',null,'{"Third-country transshipment","Shell companies","Falsified end-use statements","Third-party payments"}','{}',
'https://ofac.treasury.gov/media/931341/download?inline','US Treasury / DOJ / Commerce','verified',false,'{"typologies","red flags","transshipment"}'),

('eu-commission-edd-guidance','guidance','European Commission guidance on enhanced due diligence to prevent Russia sanctions circumvention','Guidance for EU operators: Implementing enhanced due diligence to shield against Russia sanctions circumvention',
'The Commission set out expectations for EU operators on risk assessment, enhanced due diligence and red flags for circumvention, with particular focus on common high-priority items and third-country trade.',
'The European Commission published guidance in September 2023 addressed to EU operators. It describes a risk-based approach comprising risk assessment, due diligence on counterparties and transactions, and ongoing monitoring, and lists red flags relating to transactions, business partners and the goods concerned. It is non-binding guidance; the Commission notes only the CJEU can give authoritative interpretation of EU law.',
'The guidance is aligned with the later legal "best efforts" and "No Russia" clause obligations and offers practical content for structuring compliance programmes.',
'ANALYSIS — Firms can use this guidance to evidence a reasonable compliance framework. Because it is non-binding, it should be read alongside the Regulation text and Commission FAQs.',
'2023-09-07','2023-09-07',null,(select id from jurisdictions where code='EU'),(select id from regimes where slug='russia'),(select id from authorities where slug='eu-commission'),
'{"EU operators"}','{"Trade","Manufacturing","Banking"}','{"Regulation (EU) No 833/2014"}',
null,null,null,'not_applicable',null,'{"Third-country transshipment"}','{}',
